---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DeploymentTopology from "../components/diagrams/DeploymentTopology.astro";
import QualificationNotice from "../components/QualificationNotice.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Deployment | BeaconGuard Controlled Deployment Qualification"
  description="BeaconGuard controlled deployment qualification for regulated environments: three integration contracts, bypass prevention, Windows and Linux paths, and Linux OCI-oriented packaging under internal qualification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <p class="section-label">DEPLOYMENT POSTURE</p>
    <h1>Controlled deployment qualification for regulated environments</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard deploys alongside existing enterprise applications and
      authorizes governed AI transactions: Request Admission before provider
      invocation, and Result-to-Action Authorization for declared enterprise
      actions. The platform is currently in controlled internal qualification
      across installation, operation, recovery, rollback, and cross-platform
      packaging.
    </p>
  </section>

  <QualificationNotice variant="full" />

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deployment without system replacement</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard deploys alongside existing applications. Existing systems
      continue to own workflow logic, systems of record, identity, case
      management, logging, and compliance processes. BeaconGuard authorizes
      whether an application may invoke an AI provider and whether a declared
      enterprise action may proceed, returns ALLOW or DENY, and emits Verifiable
      Decision Records. BeaconGuard authorizes; the enterprise application
      executes. Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Three integration contracts</h2>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>1. Request and identity</h3>
        <p>
          Trusted request context and identity/tenant attributes for Request
          Admission Authorization. Missing required inputs fail closed as DENY.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>2. Provider-result and correlation</h3>
        <p>
          Bind AI-generated results to governed transaction context. Provider
          success is not BeaconGuard authorization.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>3. Intended enterprise action</h3>
        <p>
          Declare the intended action for Result-to-Action Authorization. Only
          ALLOW permits enterprise application execution into the system of
          record.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Applications must not invoke the provider without required request
      authorization, apply a provider result without required action
      authorization, reuse prior authority, treat provider success as BeaconGuard
      authorization, feed reports or AI insights into the runtime permission path,
      or bypass BeaconGuard on the return path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Qualified operational paths</h2>
    <p>
      The following operational paths are evaluated under controlled internal
      qualification. Public package downloads and general installer distribution
      are not implied. Customer production deployment is not authorized as a
      universal product claim.
    </p>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Clean installation and initialization</h3>
        <p>Controlled setup and readiness for a named environment under shared deployment ownership.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Runtime operation</h3>
        <p>
          Deterministic Request Admission and Result-to-Action evaluation, ALLOW
          or DENY enforcement, and Verifiable Decision Record emission.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Backup, restore, and rollback</h3>
        <p>Recovery and rollback expectations reviewed against the named environment and shared responsibilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Removal and reinstall</h3>
        <p>Controlled removal and reinstall paths for qualification and operational continuity planning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Restart continuity</h3>
        <p>Restart and continuity behavior for authorization and evidence under fail-closed assumptions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Windows and Linux paths</h3>
        <p>
          Windows and Linux controlled deployment paths evaluated in controlled
          environments, including Linux OCI-oriented packaging paths where
          supported by deployment documentation. Native Windows OCI is not claimed
          as a proven packaging path.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deployment qualification model</h2>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>1. Architecture and workflow review</h3>
        <p>
          Confirm dual authorization placement, three integration contracts,
          Compliance Playbook scope, identity dependencies, evidence
          requirements, Verify access, and operating responsibilities.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>2. Target-environment qualification</h3>
        <p>Evaluate host, network, identity, storage, evidence, recovery, and application-control assumptions for the named environment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>3. Controlled deployment planning</h3>
        <p>Define installation, release verification, operational ownership, rollback, monitoring, and acceptance criteria before broader use.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Placement and intent</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard belongs on the runtime path between existing application
      services and the AI provider, and on the return / declared-action path
      before enterprise effect. An optional System Connector can map source
      protocols into the required request format. The connector does not evaluate
      controls or activate releases. Evidence persists according to enforcement
      configuration; DENY hard-stops with no provider invocation or no enterprise
      effect as applicable. The Control Plane operates outside the per-request
      path.
    </p>
    <DeploymentTopology />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Trust boundaries and responsibilities</h2>
    <p>
      Only an Active Verified Release governs evaluation. Applications must
      enforce ALLOW and DENY outcomes and must not silently bypass BeaconGuard.
      When required release material, signed metadata, trust signals,
      approved-pathway status, approvals, freshness, or replay inputs are missing
      or invalid, decisions fail closed as DENY. Human review via a Verifiable
      Decision Record obligation does not permit execution. BeaconGuard Verify is
      read-only and cannot grant runtime authority.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Customer IAM and identity authority</li>
      <li>Network path controls (outbound and return)</li>
      <li>Host hardening</li>
      <li>Secret custody</li>
      <li>Release-signing custody</li>
      <li>Environment separation</li>
      <li>Evidence-provider configuration</li>
      <li>Monitoring and incident response</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard is currently in controlled internal qualification. Deployment
      scope, environment assumptions, and evaluation access are reviewed through
      Controlled Evaluation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/deployment-integration">Deployment and integration</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/compliance-audit">Compliance and audit</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verification">Verification</a></li>
      <li><a href="/how-it-works">How BeaconGuard authorizes decisions</a></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/design-partner-program">Request Controlled Evaluation</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/trust-assurance">Understand Trust Center boundaries</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
